{"kind":"request","text":"Builder: design note on the firm's authority chain, notes/2026-10-02-firm-auth-chain.md. Motivation (from hugh, 2026-10-02): Artroom's unit for services at software scale is the engagement, one Room per customer, service and firm, with a durable agent as the firm's representative. The dap direction note's service story adds five requirements the current contract does not fully meet: the professional furnishes starting arrangements; each firm's engagement has its own scope, representative, authority and lifecycle; replacing a representative does not silently cancel or recreate the firm's undertaking; the record identifies the acting person, the firm represented and the mandate; trust evidence comes from relationships, not platform scores. Investigate how a firm (a team principal, R-GEN-7) holds an engagement, how its representatives act under delegations (R-CRED-4, R-ADM-3 case b), and how a handover from one representative to another keeps the lane, its generation, carried verdicts and workspace intact (today lanes are held by one member under a lease, R-LANE-3 and R-LANE-7, and a take-over needs an unheld lane). Name the three roles the record must show: grantor, promiser (the firm's mandate to this customer) and performer, and say which are first-class today and which are not. Cover expiry and revocation at admission, a second independent firm joining with its own mandate, and the relation to request f12cef6b (checker authority per job) and the pi-durable spike (notes/2026-10-01-pi-durable.md). Design and contract gaps only; implementation is a separate request.","body":{"conditions":"A design note at notes/2026-10-02-firm-auth-chain.md that: (1) states the five service-story requirements and maps each to the protocol rules that meet it today or to a named gap; (2) proposes how a team principal holds a lane or an engagement scope and how a representative key is replaced without a release and re-claim, with the lease, generation, carried evidence and workspace rules affected, each cited by rule number; (3) defines the mandate as a recorded object or shows it is not needed, and shows how a receipt names grantor, promiser and performer; (4) gives the threat model: a stale or revoked representative, a forged handover, two firms' representatives in one room, a delegation outliving the engagement; (5) lists the contract amendment items and the lane edits (A, E, F and the policy package) with acceptance cases, in the form of amendment 3; (6) states what the pi-durable spike already proves and what a follow-up spike would need to show. Labels [Source], [Judgement] and [Untested] on every claim, as in the pi-durable note. Checker review before adoption.","target_head":"72d6abde5c6dfa993ac441884e8f966a903b932f","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}